<?xml version="1.0"?>

<rotor name="mi17_tail_rotor">

    <diameter unit="FT">                  10.5 </diameter>
    <numblades>                             3 </numblades>
    <gearratio>                        4.2 </gearratio>
    <nominalrpm>                       1134.0 </nominalrpm>
    <chord unit="FT">                     0.8 </chord>
    <flappingmoment unit="SLUG*FT2">     2.5 </flappingmoment>
    <liftcurveslope>                      6.0 </liftcurveslope>
    <twist>                               0.0 </twist>
    <massmoment>                         0.8 </massmoment>
    <tiplossfactor>                       1.0 </tiplossfactor>
    <inflowlag>                          0.10 </inflowlag> 
    <hingeoffset unit="FT">             0.02 </hingeoffset>

    <!-- KNOWN LIMITATION, flagged for a later iteration; not fixed here:

         On a real Mi-17, the tail rotor is NOT independently powered. It is
         driven off the SAME combining transmission as the two main engines
         (a second power-takeoff off the main gearbox, at its own ~4.2:1
         ratio vs. the main rotor's 18.5:1), not a third engine. Physically
         it should reference either the main gearbox's combined shaft RPM
         or the engines' RPM directly; there is currently NO such
         reference anywhere in this config. This rotor instead runs on its
         own fully independent dummy engine (mi17_tail_engine, a flat 50 HP
         electric_engine) with its own separate FGTransmission; it is not
         an approximation of the shared-shaft coupling, it is simply
         decoupled physics.

         Why: the original (pre-gearbox) Mi-17 model slaved this rotor's
         RPM to engine index 0 via <ExternalRPM>0</ExternalRPM>, which
         worked when engine 0 was a normal engine with an immediately-
         available inline thruster. On this branch, engine 0
         (mi17_main_engine_1) is a gearbox-fed turboprop_engine whose
         thruster isn't assigned until FGPropulsion::Load()'s <gearbox>
         pass, which runs after every engine (including this tail engine)
         has already finished its own Load(); so FGRotor's ExternalRPM
         lookup (FGRotor.cpp, "avoid ourself and (still) unknown engines"
         branch) crashed on a null Thruster, since engine 0 is a known
         engine, just not yet thrustered at that point. <ExternalRPM> was
         removed here to stop that crash, not because the coupling
         requirement went away.

         FGGearbox (src/models/propulsion/FGGearbox.h) is documented as
         combining N engines onto ONE shared output shaft/thruster; it
         has no concept of a second, independently-geared output today.
         Properly complying with the real transmission needs either (a) a
         genuine FGGearbox extension for a second output channel sharing
         the same input torque pool, or (b) at minimum, driving this
         rotor's RPM from a property expression referencing
         propulsion/gearbox[0]/shaft-rpm scaled by the tail/main gear-ratio
         ratio, rather than an unrelated independent engine. Neither is
         done yet. -->
    <controlmap> TAIL </controlmap>

</rotor>
